{"ts": 1789784385.037837, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "{9, 4, 38} remains the winner at 1.278. Now let me calibrate my OOS forecast.\n\nThe key question is how much of that 1.278 is overfitting. Reasoning:\n\n- I ran ~70 evaluations. The winner was selected as the max over a search space of ~40 singles, then pairs/triples built greedily around them. The selection bias is real but modest, because the spec wasn't found by blind maximization over all 40\u00d739\u00d738 sign-combinations \u2014 it's the union of the three features that were *already* individually the strongest (ranks 1, 2, 3 among 40 singles). That is a much more robust construction than a top-of-search fluke.\n- Sanity check on that: the three components have standalone Sharpes of 0.984, 0.652, 0.579 \u2014 all far above the noise scale. With n=5000 daily observations, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. So each component is 3\u20134+ SEs from zero individually. These are genuine signals, not noise-mining.\n- The combination gains (1.278 vs 0.984 best single) are consistent with partially-decorrelated real signals rather than overfit \u2014 diversification of three true signals should raise Sharpe, which is exactly what I see, and the gain is sub-additive in a plausible way.\n- Working against me: selecting the max of ~40 singles inflates the top single by perhaps 0.15\u20130.25; the greedy triple search adds a bit more. And OOS regimes shift, which typically shrinks Sharpes beyond pure statistical selection bias.\n\nSo I'd shade 1.278 down meaningfully \u2014 to about 0.95 \u2014 and keep the uncertainty wide, since regime change can bite much harder than the statistics suggest."}
