{"ts": 1789784425.835237, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Result\n\n**Submitted:** long features 9, 4, and 38 (equal weight, all positive sign) \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.278** vs. search-adjusted bar of 1.020.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.95 \u00b1 0.35\n\n### How I got there\n\nI scanned all 40 features individually first, which gave a clean ranking rather than forcing me to search blindly through the ~500k possible signed triples. Three features separated clearly from the pack:\n\n| Feature | Standalone Sharpe |\n|---|---|\n| 9 | 0.984 |\n| 4 | 0.652 |\n| 38 | 0.579 |\n| 14 | 0.518 |\n| 3 | 0.408 |\n\nThe winning spec is simply the union of the top three singles. That matters for trustworthiness: it wasn't unearthed as the argmax of an exhaustive sweep, so it carries much less selection bias than a typical top-of-search result.\n\nSupporting checks:\n- **The components are real.** With n=5000, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is \u22480.22. Each of the three sits 3\u20134+ SEs above zero on its own \u2014 these aren't noise.\n- **The gains look like diversification, not overfitting.** Adding 4 to 9 lifted Sharpe 0.984 \u2192 1.162; adding 38 took it to 1.278. Sub-additive improvement is the signature of partially-decorrelated genuine signals.\n- **Sign structure is decisive.** Flipping any single component collapses the spec (1.278 \u2192 0.621, 0.520, 0.141). A fragile, overfit combination wouldn't degrade this cleanly and dramatically.\n- I tested competing triples around features 14, 3, 7, 37, 31, and 8; none beat 1.278, and the runner-up {9,4,14} at 1.266 shares two of three components \u2014 the result is stable, not perched on a spike.\n\n### On the forecast\n\nI shaded 1.278 down to 0.95 for two compounding reasons. Taking the max over 40 singles likely inflates the lead feature by ~0.15\u20130.25, and the greedy triple construction adds a little more on top. Beyond that pure statistical selection bias, OOS regime drift typically shrinks Sharpes further \u2014 and it's the larger risk here, which is why I kept sd at 0.35 rather than the ~0.22 that sampling error alone would justify. My interval comfortably admits the possibility that this lands near 0.6, while still centering on the view that these are three authentic signals."}
